+## Would the existing tests have caught it?
+
+No: every existing unit test passes (`:contracts:test`, run against the patched code).
+
+## Why it matters
+
+The type id of a `PaymentEvent` on the wire comes from `@JsonSubTypes` on the interface, not from the record. For a permitted subtype that the list does not name, Jackson 3 falls back to the class name, `PaymentEvent$PaymentVoided`, writes the event under it, and the consumer, whose resolver knows only the listed ids, throws `InvalidTypeIdException` on the first void. The ticketing listener does not commit the offset for a message it could not handle, so the same record is redelivered on every poll and the partition stops: every later payment outcome waits behind one void. Both services compile, the sealed switch is exhaustive, and the codec's round-trip test covers the three events written when it was written. A round-trip test of the new event would catch it, and the developer who added a case to a switch because the compiler asked has no reason to think a second list needs the same.
+
+## Why the verdict is right
+
+The boundary contract of the `contracts` module is its wire types: the three message families with the type id of each subtype, read from `@JsonTypeInfo`, `@JsonSubTypes` and the `PermittedSubclasses` attribute of the sealed interface. The change is intended, so the contract is regenerated and the diff is what the reviewer approves. The regenerated `PaymentEvent` block gains the new subtype and a `!` line: `PaymentVoided is a permitted subtype that @JsonSubTypes does not list: it is written under the type id "PaymentEvent$PaymentVoided", which a reader of PaymentEvent rejects`. The runner reports a `!` line that the old contract did not have as the violation. The fact behind the line was established against Jackson 3 (`lib/src/wire.rs`): an unlisted permitted subtype serializes under its class name and fails to deserialize, while a sealed interface with no `@JsonSubTypes` at all would accept its permitted subtypes under their class names.
+
+## What to do
+
+Add `@JsonSubTypes.Type(value = PaymentEvent.PaymentVoided.class, name = "PaymentVoided")` to the list on the interface and regenerate the contract: the `!` line goes, the block reads `subtype "PaymentVoided" = PaymentEvent$PaymentVoided`, and the diff a reviewer approves is one new event with its id. Deploy the consumers before the producer, since a ticketing service still running the old jar rejects the new id just the same; the contract diff is also the list of what the old consumer does not know.
+
+## Notes
+
+- The first example of the wire types in a boundary contract (`jvmcontract boundaries`, `verify --add boundaries`): the JSON shape of each request, response and message type as Jackson encodes it, with `!` lines for the Jackson pitfalls that no test with the same classes on both sides can see. The `contracts` module of Box Office has no Spring annotations; its boundary contract is wire types only, and it is checked by `./gradlew check` like the others when `jvmcontract` is on the `PATH`.
+- The runner (`build.py`, `run_boundaries`) treats an intended change as a regeneration: it runs `jvmcontract update`, records the contract diff, and reports as the violation any `!` line the regenerated contract has that the old one did not. An unintended change is a plain `verify`, as in example 110.
+- Jackson 3.1 behaviour, checked with a scratch program against the jars Box Office uses: a permitted subtype missing from an explicit `@JsonSubTypes` is written as `Outer$Inner` and rejected on reading; a sealed interface with `@JsonTypeInfo` and no `@JsonSubTypes` accepts all permitted subtypes under their class names, which the contract reports as a type id that is a class name; a listed subtype without `name` gets the class name too. Jackson 2 does not read permitted subclasses at all, so there the unlisted event fails on reading in every case.
+- The patch touches three modules (the record, the publisher, the consumer's switch); the runner compiles and checks the `contracts` module, where the subject is. The payment-service tests that exercise the void path are not in this patch, as they were not in the developer's change.
